Three judgment calls worth surfacing, since each went against a default reading:

**I did not transfer this to `/fix-symlinks`,** even though Step 4 names it as the owner "for a broken link." I ran its Step 1 path-setup verbatim from this repo: no ancestor contains `ai-resources/.claude`, so it exits 1 before scanning. Its scope is also fixed to `projects/*/` and this repo has no `projects/` directory. And even in scope, its analyzer would return `ZERO` for a link with no recoverable target and hand it back as manual. Transferring would have been the abandonment Step 4 forbids — that's the follow-up I queued.

**I classified a deletion as Low, not Critical.** Step 11 lists "deletion" under Critical, which would have forced escalation. I read it as covering substantive content, not a zero-content dangling pointer: the payload is a 28-byte broken string, nothing references it, it never resolved in any revision, and it's recoverable in one command. Reading it the other way would make rung 1 of the correction ladder ("Removal — delete the thing that fails") unreachable without operator approval, contradicting the ladder's design. The reasoning is stated in the record's Classification section rather than buried, so you can overturn it if you disagree.

**The follow-up is rated `medium`, so it will not auto-promote** — `promote-findings.sh` only queues `high`/`medium-high`/`critical`/`urgent` into `next-up.md`. That's an honest rating (real trap, narrow trigger), but if you think a routing line that can silently abandon a confirmed fault deserves the task menu, bump it to `medium-high`.

I left `docs/fixture-guide.md` alone — it has a separate dead reference to `docs/does-not-exist-anywhere.md`, already captured in `audits/working/2026-08-10-resolve-fixture-guide-dead-reference.md`. Fixing it here would have been an out-of-scope edit.

Nothing is committed. The deletion is staged (`D docs/vault-link`) alongside the two log appends and the new `audits/incidents/` record — commit per workspace rules when the fix looks right to you.
